Subject: LAT Printer appears to be overflowing it's buffer..
Date: 1996/01/18
Message-ID: <4dm83a$15l@news1.infinet.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 135732760
organization: City of Columbus, Ohio
newsgroups: comp.os.vms,vmsnet.sysmgt,comp.sys.vms

We have a HP DeskJet 1200 with 8MB RAM and an internal Emulex NetJet
print server I/O card.  A VMS 6.2 print queue is setup using the
LatSym print symbiont.  We have a project where we need to send a
bunch of scanned images to the printer.  Each image is about 1.4MB.
The problem we're having is that if we send more than about 5 at a
time to the printer, the images are scrambled.  Five images would just
about fill the printer internal memory, so it seems like the problem
is that the printer is just filling it's buffer.  However, shouldn't
the NetJet/Latsym combination keep that from happening?  I would think
that there should be some flow control to tell VMS when the printer
buffer is almost full.  The connection between the NetJet and the
printer is via the internal "MIO" port, so there's no flow control
related parameters there.  Everything in the terminal setup (/PASTHRU
and /TTYSYNC) appear to be correct, at least as far as I can tell from
the Lat docs.  Still, I'm wondering if I don't have something set up
correctly.  This is the largest set of print jobs we've sent; we've
not had any problems before this, so I've not needed to change any
default settings.

I did try breaking the print run into separate jobs of 4 images each,
but if I send the second job before the first prints, I get garbage.
We have a total of about 75 sheets to send, so 4 at a time is not the
best solution.

Any suggestions would be appreciated.




